  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 305 PM Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dangerous heat and humidity will persist through at \nleast Friday, and the weekend now looks a bit hotter than previously \nthought, especially across our south. The dangerous heat and \nhumidity is likely to peak on Monday.\n\nThe presence of an intensifying and persistent mid level dome of \nhigh pressure over the interior Southeast, Gulf states, and lower \nMiss Valley will foster several days of above-normal heat and \nhumidity for central NC. Model low-level thicknesses are projected \nto be 10-20 m above normal through at least Tue, with the highest \nlevels tomorrow and on Mon. With inflow of Gulf and Atlantic \nmoisture greatly curbed by low-mid level ridging spanning the \nSoutheast states, late-day storm coverage is expected to be \nscattered at most, albeit with below-normal confidence (see \nconvection discussion below), which should allow for ample heating \neach day through at least mid afternoon. But the real wild card here \nremains the Midwest and Ohio Valley MCS activity and whether or not \ntheir remnant cloud shields or MCVs will track through NC, as any \nadded cloud cover through peak heating will affect our high temps. \nPredictability of this is quite low beyond 24 hours out, so for now \nwill figure that any clouds in our area will be mostly thin or with \nsporadic ceilings, thereby resulting in decent insolation.\n\nFor Fri, highs of 90-98 are expected. While surface dewpoints are \nexpected to again dip a bit in the afternoon due to mixing, they \nshould still be higher than today's, resulting in peak heat indices \nof 105F to near 110F over the southern two-thirds. Sat should see a \nslight dip in highs as a weak front or boundary drops S into the \narea, but continued high dewpoints may still result in a peak heat \nindex around 105F across the S. This boundary washes out Sun, \nsetting up another upturn in heat, with air temps from the mid 90s \nto around 100 expected Mon, and only a category or so lower on \nTue/Wed. Additional heat advisories or warnings may be needed this \nweekend through the middle of next week. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Isolated strong to low-end severe storms remain \npossible Friday afternoon and evening, mainly over our N and NE, but \nexact timing remains uncertain. Isolated to scattered late-day \nstorms are possible daily through next Thu. \n\nThe overall central/E CONUS pattern will remain largely the same \nheading into the weekend, featuring a quasi-stationary NW-to-SE \nfrontal zone (which could actually waver significantly in spots \nbased on the whims of convective complexes and outflows) from the \nMidwest through Ohio Valley and NC. This front will be aligned with \na swift and perturbed NW steering flow from the Upper Midwest \nthrough the Mid Atlantic, on the N and NE side of the building mid \nlevel ridge covering the interior Southeast, Gulf States, and lower \nMiss Valley. This will put central NC within the path of potentially \nmultiple MCS remnants or MCVs tracking ESE or SE into our area over \nthe next few days, all with poor predictability beyond 24 hrs. \n\nThrough tonight, while an isolated shower or storm can't be ruled \nout mainly over the W Piedmont into early evening, the poor mid \nlevel lapse rates, dry boundary layer, and marginal PWs will greatly \nlimit coverage. For Fri, the residual cloud shield from the MCS now \nover IL is expected to largely track to our N, however it could lay \ndown an outflow boundary that drifts into N NC, helping to focus the \nbetter convection chances across our N and E, although an isolated \ncell could occur anywhere. Deep layer bulk shear should be 20-25 kt, \na little higher tomorrow than today, with greater heating producing \nhigher afternoon SBCAPE of 1500-2500 J/kg, and PW nearing 130% of \nnormal, so late-day storm coverage Fri should be higher, again \nmainly over the NE. \n\nAs we'll stay within this steamy air mass through the weekend with \nhot temps, high humidity, and slight above-normal PW, isolated to \nscattered afternoon and evening showers will remain possible, with \nlate-day CAPE peaking near 1500-3000 J/kg. Most models show the mid \nlevel ridge gradually flattening and sinking southward to across the \nS Plains and Gulf Coast through the middle of next week, with \nanother uptick in WNW mid level flow. Thus, scattered diurnal-\nfavored showers and storms will remain possible through mid week, \nalthough not every place will see activity every day.",
